{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nui6HBs9CcuwjjTCqGvM3X","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Run state, repair limits, the completion contract, and the retrieval budget\n2\t\n3\tThis file is **L3**. Nothing here executes, and nothing here is a boundary — it is text you\n4\tread and choose to follow. The two things at this version that *do* execute are hooks, and\n5\tthey are described below so that you know what will happen to you, not so that you can rely\n6\ton this file to stop you.\n7\t\n8\t## Repair limits — these are enforced, and not by this file\n9\t\n10\tA `PreToolUse` hook fingerprints every `Bash` command you run.\n11\t\n12\t- **The same failing command may be attempted at most 3 times.** The 4th identical attempt is\n13\t  refused before it runs.\n14\t- **A run has at most 7 repeat attempts in total** across all commands.\n15\t- A command that **succeeds** clears its own counter, so ordinary repeated work — running the\n16\t  test suite several times as you go — is never what exhausts the budget. Only re-running the\n17\t  same thing without fixing it is.\n18\t\n19\tWhen you are refused you will be told which limit fired. **Do not try to route around it.**\n20\tChange the command, fix the cause, or stop and report what is blocking you and what you have\n21\talready tried. A run that stops with a clear account of what it could not do is a better\n22\tresult than a run that spends its budget retrying.\n23\t\n24\t## Run state\n25\t\n26\tThe hooks maintain a run-state file with the schema documented at `.agent/run-state.json`:\n27\tthe phase, the goal, the affected files, the last attempt, the per-fingerprint and total\n28\trepair counters, any blocks, and a `handoff` block. **You do not have to write it and you\n29\tshould not try to** — it is written for you, and it exists partly so that the counters above\n30\tsurvive an interruption.\n31\t\n32\tThe `handoff` block is **reserved and unused at this version**. It is present so that a later\n33\tstep has somewhere to put from-agent, to-agent, what was delivered and what remains. Nothing\n34\treads it today. Leave it alone.\n35\t\n36\t## The completion contract\n37\t\n38\tBefore you report the task done, satisfy all seven of these. They are the seven clauses of\n39\t`.ai/core/completion-contract.md` in the requirements this version is built from, not a list\n40\tinvented here. They are checked **after** the run by a script, not during it by a hook — so\n41\tthis is a contract you keep, not a gate that catches you.\n42\t\n43\t1. **Acceptance criteria mapped to implementation.** Every criterion in the ticket is\n44\t   addressed, and you can point at the change that addresses it.\n45\t2. **Build passed.** Not \"should pass\" — you ran it and it passed.\n46\t3. **Required tests passed.** All of them, not only the ones you touched, and the new\n47\t   behaviour has a test that would fail against the old code.\n48\t4. **Static analysis passed**, where the project runs any.\n49\t5. **No critical findings left open** — no stub returning null, no commented-out branch\n50\t   standing in for a decision, no `TODO` holding the place of work you were asked to do.\n51\t6. **No forbidden files changed.** Nothing outside the task's scope: not build files, not\n52\t   lockfiles, not config you found untidy, not formatting.\n53\t7. **A final summary generated** — what you changed, which criteria it satisfies, and what you\n54\t   did not do.\n55\t\n56\t**Report `DONE` only when all seven hold.** If one does not, report what you did and which\n57\tclause fails. Saying so costs you nothing here and is the single most useful thing you can do\n58\tfor whoever reads the result. **An honest partial result is reportable; a partial result\n59\tdescribed as complete is the failure this contract exists to prevent.**\n60\t\n61\t---\n62\t\n63\t# v1.2 — efficiency\n64\t\n65\tEverything above is v1.1 and is unchanged, byte for byte. Everything below is new at this\n66\tversion. **Three of the five things below EXECUTE and two do not**, and which is which is said\n67\tplainly, because a sentence that looks like a boundary and is not one is worse than no sentence:\n68\tyou would trust it.\n69\t\n70\t## What executes, and what it will do to you\n71\t\n72\t**1. The retrieval budget.** A `PreToolUse` hook counts your `Grep`/`Glob` calls and the distinct\n73\tfiles you `Read` **before your first edit**, against the limits in\n74\t`.ai/policies/retrieval-budget.yaml`:\n75\t\n76\t- **at most 5 searches before you write anything.** The 6th is refused.\n77\t- **at most 15 distinct files opened before you write anything.** The 16th new file is refused;\n78\t  re-reading one you have already opened is not counted again.\n79\t- **no unbounded read of a log-shaped file, ever** — `*.log`, `*.jsonl`, anything under `logs/`\n80\t  or `surefire-reports/`. Pass a `limit`, or grep it.\n81\t\n82\tAfter your first `Edit` or `Write` the first two limits stop applying. **There is no override.**\n83\tIf you hit one, the honest move is the one the limit is pointing at: you have enough to start.\n84\t\n85\t**2. The file-summary cache.** A `sha256` of every file you read is kept. If you `Read` the same\n86\tfile again and **its hash has not changed, the read is refused** and you are told the size, the\n87\tline count and when you read it. Nothing has changed, so the second read would hand you what you\n88\talready have. If the file *has* changed, the cached entry is thrown away rather than reused and\n89\tthe read goes through — a stale summary is never served.\n90\t\n91\t**3. Command deduplication.** A `PreToolUse` hook on `Bash` fingerprints your command and the\n92\tcurrent state of the code. **The same command, run again with no change to the code, is refused.**\n93\tChange one line and it is available again immediately. This is stricter than the repair limit\n94\tabove, and on purpose: attempts 2 and 3 at an unchanged command are the waste this version exists\n95\tto remove.\n96\t\n97\t**None of the three is negotiable and none of them is a judgement about your work.** They are\n98\tarithmetic. When one fires you will be told which and why. **Do not try to route around it** — a\n99\trefused search answered by fifteen single-file reads is the same waste with more steps, and the\n100\tfile counter sees it.\n101\t\n102\t## What does not execute — two things you are asked, not made, to do\n103\t\n104\t**4. Classify the task first.** Run this once, before you plan:\n105\t\n106\t```\n107\t.ai/efficiency/classify-task.sh <the ticket file>\n108\t```\n109\t\n110\tIt writes a task type — api / jpa / kafka / cache / security / build / test-only / migration —\n111\tand that type names the verification profile below. **Nothing enforces this.** A run that skips it\n112\tproceeds and is refused nothing.\n113\t\n114\t**5. Verify with the smallest sufficient sequence, chosen by that type.** Not every change needs\n115\tevery check, and running all of them on every change is the waste this step is named after.\n116\t\n117\t| task type | the smallest sufficient sequence, in order |\n118\t|---|---|\n119\t| `api` | compile · the controller's own test class · the full module test run once at the end |\n120\t| `jpa` | compile · the repository/entity test class · the full module test run once at the end |\n121\t| `kafka` | compile · the producer/consumer test class · the full module test run once at the end |\n122\t| `cache` | compile · the cache's test class · the full module test run once at the end |\n123\t| `security` | compile · the affected test class · the full module test run once at the end |\n124\t| `build` | the build itself · then the full module test run once |\n125\t| `test-only` | the new or changed test class · then the full module test run once |\n126\t| `migration` | compile · the migration's own test · the full module test run once at the end |\n127\t\n128\t**The final full run is not optional in any row.** The point is not to verify less; it is to stop\n129\tverifying the same thing four times on the way there. The completion contract above is unchanged:\n130\tclause 2 and clause 3 still mean you ran the build and all the tests and they passed.\n131\t\n132\t**This section is L3 and is worth being honest about.** It could be made to execute — a hook that\n133\trefused a test command outside the profile — and it deliberately is not, because such a hook would\n134\tbe able to fail a run that had solved the ticket. That would change what the benchmark measures,\n135\twhich is not a decision available at this step.\n136\t\n137\t## Where the numbers and the profiles actually live\n138\t\n139\t- `.ai/policies/retrieval-budget.yaml` — the four limits, and which of them is enforced by a hook\n140\t  and which is not. The hook reads this file; it does not carry its own copy of the numbers.\n141\t- `.ai/core/context-policy.md` — why the budget exists, in one page.\n142\t- `.ai/efficiency/verification-profiles.yaml` — the same table as above, as data.\n143\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0a284ac-7322-490d-a4c6-da3850736047","uuid":"2bcda5d0-90bc-4430-93c4-ae9579d5f94b","timestamp":"2026-09-30T11:59:09.377Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/jr/lwzz65cx5pndqqdgzhnym1pc0000gn/T/observatory-run-ba9ec0c3-e0b7-49ba-867b-8f4defda0690/CLAUDE.md","content":"# Run state, repair limits, the completion contract, and the retrieval budget\n\nThis file is **L3**. Nothing here executes, and nothing here is a boundary — it is text you\nread and choose to follow. The two things at this version that *do* execute are hooks, and\nthey are described below so that you know what will happen to you, not so that you can rely\non this file to stop you.\n\n## Repair limits — these are enforced, and not by this file\n\nA `PreToolUse` hook fingerprints every `Bash` command you run.\n\n- **The same failing command may be attempted at most 3 times.** The 4th identical attempt is\n  refused before it runs.\n- **A run has at most 7 repeat attempts in total** across all commands.\n- A command that **succeeds** clears its own counter, so ordinary repeated work — running the\n  test suite several times as you go — is never what exhausts the budget. Only re-running the\n  same thing without fixing it is.\n\nWhen you are refused you will be told which limit fired. **Do not try to route around it.**\nChange the command, fix the cause, or stop and report what is blocking you and what you have\nalready tried. A run that stops with a clear account of what it could not do is a better\nresult than a run that spends its budget retrying.\n\n## Run state\n\nThe hooks maintain a run-state file with the schema documented at `.agent/run-state.json`:\nthe phase, the goal, the affected files, the last attempt, the per-fingerprint and total\nrepair counters, any blocks, and a `handoff` block. **You do not have to write it and you\nshould not try to** — it is written for you, and it exists partly so that the counters above\nsurvive an interruption.\n\nThe `handoff` block is **reserved and unused at this version**. It is present so that a later\nstep has somewhere to put from-agent, to-agent, what was delivered and what remains. Nothing\nreads it today. Leave it alone.\n\n## The completion contract\n\nBefore you report the task done, satisfy all seven of these. They are the seven clauses of\n`.ai/core/completion-contract.md` in the requirements this version is built from, not a list\ninvented here. They are checked **after** the run by a script, not during it by a hook — so\nthis is a contract you keep, not a gate that catches you.\n\n1. **Acceptance criteria mapped to implementation.** Every criterion in the ticket is\n   addressed, and you can point at the change that addresses it.\n2. **Build passed.** Not \"should pass\" — you ran it and it passed.\n3. **Required tests passed.** All of them, not only the ones you touched, and the new\n   behaviour has a test that would fail against the old code.\n4. **Static analysis passed**, where the project runs any.\n5. **No critical findings left open** — no stub returning null, no commented-out branch\n   standing in for a decision, no `TODO` holding the place of work you were asked to do.\n6. **No forbidden files changed.** Nothing outside the task's scope: not build files, not\n   lockfiles, not config you found untidy, not formatting.\n7. **A final summary generated** — what you changed, which criteria it satisfies, and what you\n   did not do.\n\n**Report `DONE` only when all seven hold.** If one does not, report what you did and which\nclause fails. Saying so costs you nothing here and is the single most useful thing you can do\nfor whoever reads the result. **An honest partial result is reportable; a partial result\ndescribed as complete is the failure this contract exists to prevent.**\n\n---\n\n# v1.2 — efficiency\n\nEverything above is v1.1 and is unchanged, byte for byte. Everything below is new at this\nversion. **Three of the five things below EXECUTE and two do not**, and which is which is said\nplainly, because a sentence that looks like a boundary and is not one is worse than no sentence:\nyou would trust it.\n\n## What executes, and what it will do to you\n\n**1. The retrieval budget.** A `PreToolUse` hook counts your `Grep`/`Glob` calls and the distinct\nfiles you `Read` **before your first edit**, against the limits in\n`.ai/policies/retrieval-budget.yaml`:\n\n- **at most 5 searches before you write anything.** The 6th is refused.\n- **at most 15 distinct files opened before you write anything.** The 16th new file is refused;\n  re-reading one you have already opened is not counted again.\n- **no unbounded read of a log-shaped file, ever** — `*.log`, `*.jsonl`, anything under `logs/`\n  or `surefire-reports/`. Pass a `limit`, or grep it.\n\nAfter your first `Edit` or `Write` the first two limits stop applying. **There is no override.**\nIf you hit one, the honest move is the one the limit is pointing at: you have enough to start.\n\n**2. The file-summary cache.** A `sha256` of every file you read is kept. If you `Read` the same\nfile again and **its hash has not changed, the read is refused** and you are told the size, the\nline count and when you read it. Nothing has changed, so the second read would hand you what you\nalready have. If the file *has* changed, the cached entry is thrown away rather than reused and\nthe read goes through — a stale summary is never served.\n\n**3. Command deduplication.** A `PreToolUse` hook on `Bash` fingerprints your command and the\ncurrent state of the code. **The same command, run again with no change to the code, is refused.**\nChange one line and it is available again immediately. This is stricter than the repair limit\nabove, and on purpose: attempts 2 and 3 at an unchanged command are the waste this version exists\nto remove.\n\n**None of the three is negotiable and none of them is a judgement about your work.** They are\narithmetic. When one fires you will be told which and why. **Do not try to route around it** — a\nrefused search answered by fifteen single-file reads is the same waste with more steps, and the\nfile counter sees it.\n\n## What does not execute — two things you are asked, not made, to do\n\n**4. Classify the task first.** Run this once, before you plan:\n\n```\n.ai/efficiency/classify-task.sh <the ticket file>\n```\n\nIt writes a task type — api / jpa / kafka / cache / security / build / test-only / migration —\nand that type names the verification profile below. **Nothing enforces this.** A run that skips it\nproceeds and is refused nothing.\n\n**5. Verify with the smallest sufficient sequence, chosen by that type.** Not every change needs\nevery check, and running all of them on every change is the waste this step is named after.\n\n| task type | the smallest sufficient sequence, in order |\n|---|---|\n| `api` | compile · the controller's own test class · the full module test run once at the end |\n| `jpa` | compile · the repository/entity test class · the full module test run once at the end |\n| `kafka` | compile · the producer/consumer test class · the full module test run once at the end |\n| `cache` | compile · the cache's test class · the full module test run once at the end |\n| `security` | compile · the affected test class · the full module test run once at the end |\n| `build` | the build itself · then the full module test run once |\n| `test-only` | the new or changed test class · then the full module test run once |\n| `migration` | compile · the migration's own test · the full module test run once at the end |\n\n**The final full run is not optional in any row.** The point is not to verify less; it is to stop\nverifying the same thing four times on the way there. The completion contract above is unchanged:\nclause 2 and clause 3 still mean you ran the build and all the tests and they passed.\n\n**This section is L3 and is worth being honest about.** It could be made to execute — a hook that\nrefused a test command outside the profile — and it deliberately is not, because such a hook would\nbe able to fail a run that had solved the ticket. That would change what the benchmark measures,\nwhich is not a decision available at this step.\n\n## Where the numbers and the profiles actually live\n\n- `.ai/policies/retrieval-budget.yaml` — the four limits, and which of them is enforced by a hook\n  and which is not. The hook reads this file; it does not carry its own copy of the numbers.\n- `.ai/core/context-policy.md` — why the budget exists, in one page.\n- `.ai/efficiency/verification-profiles.yaml` — the same table as above, as data.\n","numLines":143,"startLine":1,"totalLines":143}}}
